ID: B3-Ch08a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch8_defeat,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_detected,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_heartfelt,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_king,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_next,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_overseer,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_overseer2,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_precisely,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_theory,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_three,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_voice,0)
set(v_b3_ch8_watching,0)
 

"Let me make sure I have this straight," Daren says. We are standing in the center of the underground quarters where we slept last night. "These blue crystals in the rock—the ones humming and lighting up every few minutes—are crystallized Magium essence?"

"Yes," Arraka says from the amulet. "That is what I just said."

"And by drawing on the Magium's power, the crystals erased the fact that Gontrok ever had five fingers on his left hand?" Daren turns to me, his brow furrowed. "That is what you are telling me, Barry?"

"It is more complicated than that," I say, and walk him through the specifics: what the pressure in your skull feels like when the fabric of the world starts coming apart, and how close Leila and I came to nonexistence during both direct encounters with the Magium.

Two hours have passed since Albert teleported us back to the rebel base. By the time we returned from the seredium tower, the rebels and the underground king's workers had already cleared the heaviest rubble. Earth elementalists reinforced the cavern ceilings cracked by royal guard bombardments, leaving little for us to haul. Once we delivered news of the king of Ollendor's death, Daren tended to the wounded civilians in the tunnels, and then we retreated to our quarters to make sense of what happened and decide our next move.

Through all of it, Melindra has not stopped pacing. Her enchanter's oath bound her to lead us toward the pinecone detector the moment the king of Ollendor died, unless we explicitly called for a rest. Breaking that vow carries a lethal backlash. Even with our repeated assurances that we are on a break, she keeps testing her collar like an animal in a snare.

"Right," Daren says when I finish explaining the void and the erasure.

He rubs the bridge of his nose, working to reconcile the impossible mechanics with common sense. "Right. So it is not just that our memories failed—the concept itself was scrubbed from the universe. And the only reason you kept hold of it is lessathi blood giving you an inherent link to the Magium. Meaning, in this room, only you, Leila, doctor Reidzek, and Melindra remember."

"We established that," Melindra snaps, tugging at the sleeve of her silk tunic. "The four of us remember five fingers, the rest of you remember three. Can we walk out of this city now? Every minute we stand here, the oath tightens around my lungs."

"Melindra," Daren says, exasperation creeping into his voice. "The oath cannot strangle you without giving both of us an aura warning first. That is panic, not enchanter magic. Breathe. You told us reaching your friend with the detector takes days. We are going to stop for water, meals, and sleep along the road. Are you going to lose your mind at every campsite?"

"That is different," Melindra says. "There is no reason to linger in this hole. The king is dead. What else keeps us here?"

"I am not stepping an inch past those tunnels until I understand why these crystals are reacting, and what danger they pose to us," Daren says. "Barry and Leila heard Eiden warn the king about the catastrophe of shattering them. Arraka, what was he talking about?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Intro2
TEXT:

"Why ask me?" Arraka says. "Do I look like a lapidary to you?"

"Yes," Daren says, his voice rising. "You are the one who identified the material in the first place."

"The only practical use for those stones is stabilizing complicated aura modifications inside magical devices," Arraka says. "I am neither a gnome nor a lessathi tinkerer. Machinery bores me, which makes my knowledge of their engineering applications remarkably thin. You already have the summary."

"On the stones, perhaps," Daren says, his jaw setting. "On the Magium itself, you give us nothing."

"Do you think I intend to invite total erasure just to indulge your curiosity?" Arraka snaps. "You have enough facts to piece together the outline, especially after Barry and Leila's excursion. I do not possess an exhaustive encyclopedia either. For ages I considered the Magium a passive backdrop, barely worth tracking. These recent disruptions suggest an active presence far beyond my prior models. Analyzing that takes time and research, neither of which progress well while I am stuffed inside gold plating."

"Right," Daren says, caught off guard. "If even you are operating on guesswork, our odds of solving it are slim. Still, the baseline is clear: it unknits matter and memory, and a sliver of its will can unravel a universe. Yet it remains fallible. You and the God of Fate slipped past it during Selkram's ritual using a suggestion spell."

"What?" Arraka shrieks. "A suggestion spell on the Magium? Have you lost your mind?"

"Barry said that was how you described it," Daren says. "Did I misunderstand?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Intro3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Intro3)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Intro3
TEXT:

"What I told Barry," Arraka says, "was that if he insisted on a crude analogy, the technique resembled an absurdly dense suggestion weave rather than an illusion. It was not a spell. How could standard spellcraft touch a force that dismantles reality at a whim?"

"If not magic," Daren asks, "what did you cast?"

"How am I supposed to explain the mechanics when you lack the basic—"

The amulet goes quiet.

"No," Arraka says. "Forget traditional instruction. If I am to forge you clowns into weapons against the God of Time and the rest of the pantheon that cast me out, I must adapt. If I cannot distill metaphysical architecture down to terms an inebriated primate can grasp, I have failed as an educator."

"We understand," Hadrik grunts. "We are drunken apes. Get to the point before—"

"And once the apes comprehend the lesson," Arraka continues over him, "I will simplify it one step further for your specific benefit, dwarf, and then we might see progress."

"Now hold on—" Hadrik starts.

"Are you going to answer the question?" Daren asks, staring down the gold casing.

"Fine," Arraka says. "In crude terms: I channeled a primordial discipline dating from before the three planes separated. I call it magic only because your vocabulary offers no alternative; standard casting cannot touch it."

"Hold on," Daren says, holding up a hand. "Are you saying the earthen, magical, and ethereal planes were once a single construct? How do you know that? You were not alive before the split, were you?"

"I was not, but the evidence remains if you know how to read the weave," Arraka says. "The survival of primordial technique is proof enough. The core problem is simple: influencing the Magium is impossible unless you strike across all three planes at once. Primordial methods disregard planar divisions entirely."

"All three planes at once," Daren murmurs. He looks over at me. "Barry, you and Leila said you were being hurled across the planes while the entity tore at the workshop, correct?"

"Every plane simultaneously," I say. Leila nods beside me, confirming the sensation.

"Then if it casts you across planes and only answers to forces spanning all three," Daren says, "the Magium must tie directly to that primordial state before the division. I see no other logical model. What do the rest of you make of it?"

choice(""What if the Magium is the primordial plane itself?"", B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_b3_ch8_theory = 0)
choice(""Maybe the Magium is like an ancient guardian, tasked with protecting the fabric of existence."", B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_b3_ch8_theory = 1)
choice(""What if the Magium is everything and nothing at the same time?"", B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_b3_ch8_theory = 2)
choice(""Maybe the Magium is the reason why the planes were separated in the first place."", B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Theory, v_b3_ch8_theory = 3)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Theory
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_theory == 0) {
"What if the Magium is the primordial plane itself?"

"A plane of existence possessing intent?" Daren asks. "Given what we have witnessed, I cannot dismiss it outright. But would that not mean our earthen plane is still part of it? Why rely on seredium crystals to reach into our world?"

"The crystals might be relics from before the division," I say. "Artifacts that retain a cleaner resonance with the source than ordinary stone. I am guessing, of course. Does anyone have a different angle?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_theory == 1) {
"Maybe the Magium is like an ancient guardian, tasked with protecting the fabric of existence."

"Like an advanced construct?" Daren asks. "An Overseer on a cosmic scale?"

"Built by whatever civilizations inhabited the primordial realm," I suggest.

"That raises contradictions," Daren says. "Arraka mentioned a aspect of the Magium handles the generation of spirits on the magical plane. Why program a custodian for planar functions before the magical plane existed?"

"It could have adapted after the rupture," I say. "Rewritten its parameters when the world fractured. Still, it is pure speculation. Anyone else want to weigh in?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_theory == 2) {
"What if the Magium is everything and nothing at the same time?"

"Barry, philosophical paradoxes do not give us a workable plan," Daren says. "If the Magium encompassed all things, it would not have needed to interrogate us regarding the whereabouts of the alternate Arraka. Boundless power aside, it clearly operates under constraints. Otherwise it would not deploy time weavers and intermediaries to execute tasks."

"Arraka claimed the presence we faced was merely a fragment of its will," I say. "Still, it was only a thought. If someone has a cleaner theory, speak up."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_theory == 3) {
"Maybe the Magium is the reason why the planes were separated in the first place."

"What leads you there?" Daren asks.

"Its reach across the current structure," I say. "It seeds new spirits into the magical plane, and sends time weavers onto the earthen plane with warnings before catastrophes. With that level of administrative control, it might have engineered the separation itself."

"That is a leap," Daren says. "By the same token, the fracture could have been a catastrophic accident, leaving the Magium to shore up the ruins. Guessing at events tens of thousands of years in the past from a few offhand remarks will not give us solid ground. Other theories?"

}
"Listen," Hadrik says, lifting both hands to cut in. "What if most of the creatures on that primordial plane perished when the world broke apart, except for one monstrous entity too stubborn to die, and that survivor became the Magium?"

"An ancient counterpart to Arraka?" Daren asks. "A predator that consumed every other entity until nothing could kill it?"

"Exactly," Hadrik says, nodding firmly.

"It would explain why it pursues Arraka with such venom," Daren says. "A reflection of its own origin."

"You are overlooking the scale," Kate says, stepping forward. "Multiple iterations of Arraka exist across parallel universes, yet the evidence points to a single Magium. Arraka navigated through its domain to jump between timelines during Selkram's ritual, meaning the Magium serves as an axis connecting all realities. It arrived in our workshop pursuing a version of Arraka from a different universe entirely. If a survivor from the primordial plane survived the collapse, it had to transcend time and space altogether, fashioning an expanse between worlds where natural laws break down—"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Boundaries, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Boundaries)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Boundaries
TEXT:

"Enough!" Hadrik groans, dragging both hands through his beard and hair. "My skull is throbbing. How are you following any of this? Can we talk about something practical? We do not need a lecture on cosmic origin. I want to know how to duck when it swings. How did the alternate Arraka stay hidden if this thing knows everything? Why did it need our directions to track her down?"

"She concealed the dimensional coordinates of her home universe," Arraka says. "A standard precaution. I would have done the same."

"And you handed those coordinates over," Daren says. "Does that mean she is off the board for the next ritual?"

"Hardly," Arraka sneers. "An Arraka does not perish that easily, even an inferior copy. Coordinates are only one layer of evasion. When cornered, she will burn through every trick in her arsenal. If you want my assessment, we will face her again in the next trial, and she will be considerably less pleasant. Best of luck to you all! Aha-Ahahahahaha!"

"Splendid," Daren mutters. "And when the Magium realizes the coordinates yielded nothing, will it return here to settle the score?"

"No," Arraka says. "The entity pulled the truth straight from my memory; it knows I did not fabricate the data. Having extracted what it needed, it resumed the hunt across the void, exactly as it was doing before colliding with us."

"And if another manifestation drops into our lap?" Daren presses. "What then? Do we freeze in place and hope you pull another miracle out of the ether?"

"Daren is right," Kate says. "Depending entirely on your intervention is not a plan. We need to understand how primordial techniques operate. How does one influence a force that exists outside temporal flow?"

"The Magium in its entirety remains beyond time, but that is not how it engages mortals," Arraka says. "When it reaches into the planes, it acts through localized projections of intent. I call them fragments. These offshoots function with narrow mandates and operate within the flow of time, just as we do. That temporal anchor gives us leverage. Both incursions you survived were directed by fragments, regardless of what shorthand I used before."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Potential, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Potential)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Potential
TEXT:

"For someone terrified of getting unmade five minutes ago, you are suddenly very talkative," Hadrik remarks. "Where did the sudden generosity come from?"

"The boundary lies at revealing its fundamental nature to the uninitiated," Arraka says. "There are three specific words I will not utter. Those three words locked Flower and Illuna's tongues, and nearly wiped us during Selkram's trial. So long as I avoid that specific definition, analyzing fragments and mechanics while standing outside the Magium's realm carries negligible risk."

"Good to know," Daren says. "Then on the subject of primordial techniques: who else on this plane or beyond can cast them, aside from you and the God of Fate?"

"Most deities and high-ranking spirits on the magical plane possess the capacity," Arraka says, "though I have not polled them in five millennia. On the earthen plane? Eiden, almost certainly. Perhaps that old sage, Azarius. Both have the intellect, reserves, and insolence to reconstruct the theory, despite their infancy compared to ancient spirits."

"What about our circle?" Kate asks. "Among the people in this room, who possesses the greatest potential to master primordial casting?"

"In this room?" The amulet vibrates with outrage. "Are you out of your mind?"

"I am asking strictly about potential," Kate says evenly. "If you had no alternative and were forced to train one person here, who would it be?"

"Strictly potential..." Arraka mutters, the silence dragging out as she searches for any acceptable loophole. "If forced under absolute duress..."

She hesitates, the syllables practically choking in her throat.

"Barry," Arraka says, dripping with revulsion. "Barry is the only viable candidate."

choice(""Obviously, this would be the only logical choice. I was expecting you to say that."", B3-Ch08a-Logical, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Logical)
choice(""It felt like you were about to puke when you said those last words. Are you alright?"", B3-Ch08a-Puke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Puke)
choice(""Hey, thanks for the endorsement! I never knew you had such a high opinion of me!"", B3-Ch08a-Endorsement, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Endorsement)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Logical
TEXT:

#if(v_bluff == 0) {
"Obviously, this would be the only logical choice. I was expecting you to say that."

"Your aura is fluttering, idiot," Arraka says. "You expected nothing of the sort."

"Why Barry?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't primordial casting require an actual mage?"

"Of course it does," Arraka snaps. "That is why unlocking his magical stats remains our primary objective."

"We have no idea when that happens," Hadrik says. "Wouldn't training an active caster be faster?"

"No," Arraka says. "Primordial manipulation demands comprehensive mastery of magical theory across every discipline—a foundation the rest of you lack entirely. Barry is the only generalist in the party. Even with his current inability to cast, he remains the most suitable candidate by a mile. Furthermore, his lessathi heritage gives him the resonance required to bridge a Magium fragment once his channels open."

}
#if(v_bluff >= 1) {
"Obviously, this would be the only logical choice. I was expecting you to say that."

"Your aura gives you away, idiot," Arraka says. "Even leaning on your bluff stat, you are completely transparent."

"Why Barry?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't primordial casting require an actual mage?"

"Of course it does," Arraka snaps. "That is why unlocking his magical stats remains our primary objective."

"We have no idea when that happens," Hadrik says. "Wouldn't training an active caster be faster?"

"No," Arraka says. "Primordial manipulation demands comprehensive mastery of magical theory across every discipline—a foundation the rest of you lack entirely. Barry is the only generalist in the party. Even with his current inability to cast, he remains the most suitable candidate by a mile. Furthermore, his lessathi heritage gives him the resonance required to bridge a Magium fragment once his channels open."

}
#if(v_bluff == 0 || v_bluff >= 1) {
"Once his channels open," Daren echoes. "Doctor Reidzek could not force the lock, and the cipher is nowhere to be found. How do we achieve that before the tournament concludes?"

"If brute force fails," Arraka says, "you locate a high-ranking lessathi who holds the cipher and extract it by force. Must I diagram every basic step?"

"Easier said than done," Daren says. "None of us are on speaking terms with lessathi leadership. Kate, Leila, and doctor Reidzek are marked outcasts, and no one else here has connections to—Melindra, you are awfully quiet. Is something wrong?"

"Nothing," Melindra says quickly, her posture stiffening. "Everything is fine."

Daren studies her for a second.

"Leave it," I tell him quietly. "If high-ranking lessathi are holding her brother, pushing for details could put him in danger."

"Fair point," Daren says, nodding. "Melindra, whatever the trouble with your brother, let us know if you need our help."

"Right," Melindra says, looking away. "If anything comes up."

"Is anyone else getting the feeling we are being watched?" Flower asks, pointing toward the wall where several blue crystals pulse with sudden light.

"Watched through the stone?" Daren asks, glancing at the rock face. "The Magium would hardly bother spying in secret when it can manifest directly."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Observing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Observing)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Puke
TEXT:

"It felt like you were about to puke when you said those last words. Are you alright?"

"No, I am not alright!" Arraka snarls. "The words were toxic coming out of my mouth. I feel ill!"

"Why Barry?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't primordial casting require an actual mage?"

"Of course it does," Arraka snaps. "That is why unlocking his magical stats remains our primary objective."

"We have no idea when that happens," Hadrik says. "Wouldn't training an active caster be faster?"

"No," Arraka says. "Primordial manipulation demands comprehensive mastery of magical theory across every discipline—a foundation the rest of you lack entirely. Barry is the only generalist in the party. Even with his current inability to cast, he remains the most suitable candidate by a mile. Furthermore, his lessathi heritage gives him the resonance required to bridge a Magium fragment once his channels open."

"Once his channels open," Daren echoes. "Doctor Reidzek could not force the lock, and the cipher is nowhere to be found. How do we achieve that before the tournament concludes?"

"If brute force fails," Arraka says, "you locate a high-ranking lessathi who holds the cipher and extract it by force. Must I diagram every basic step?"

"Easier said than done," Daren says. "None of us are on speaking terms with lessathi leadership. Kate, Leila, and doctor Reidzek are marked outcasts, and no one else here has connections to—Melindra, you are awfully quiet. Is something wrong?"

"Nothing," Melindra says quickly, her posture stiffening. "Everything is fine."

Daren studies her for a second.

"Leave it," I tell him quietly. "If high-ranking lessathi are holding her brother, pushing for details could put him in danger."

"Fair point," Daren says, nodding. "Melindra, whatever the trouble with your brother, let us know if you need our help."

"Right," Melindra says, looking away. "If anything comes up."

"Is anyone else getting the feeling we are being watched?" Flower asks, pointing toward the wall where several blue crystals pulse with sudden light.

"Watched through the stone?" Daren asks, glancing at the rock face. "The Magium would hardly bother spying in secret when it can manifest directly."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Observing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Observing)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Endorsement
TEXT:

"Hey, thanks for the endorsement! I never knew you had such a high opinion of me!"

"I have no opinion of you, you insect!" Arraka shrieks. "You were simply the least disastrous entry on an abysmal list. In any civilized setting you would not make the parchment!"

"Why Barry?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't primordial casting require an actual mage?"

"Of course it does," Arraka snaps. "That is why unlocking his magical stats remains our primary objective."

"We have no idea when that happens," Hadrik says. "Wouldn't training an active caster be faster?"

"No," Arraka says. "Primordial manipulation demands comprehensive mastery of magical theory across every discipline—a foundation the rest of you lack entirely. Barry is the only generalist in the party. Even with his current inability to cast, he remains the most suitable candidate by a mile. Furthermore, his lessathi heritage gives him the resonance required to bridge a Magium fragment once his channels open."

"Once his channels open," Daren echoes. "Doctor Reidzek could not force the lock, and the cipher is nowhere to be found. How do we achieve that before the tournament concludes?"

"If brute force fails," Arraka says, "you locate a high-ranking lessathi who holds the cipher and extract it by force. Must I diagram every basic step?"

"Easier said than done," Daren says. "None of us are on speaking terms with lessathi leadership. Kate, Leila, and doctor Reidzek are marked outcasts, and no one else here has connections to—Melindra, you are awfully quiet. Is something wrong?"

"Nothing," Melindra says quickly, her posture stiffening. "Everything is fine."

Daren studies her for a second.

"Leave it," I tell him quietly. "If high-ranking lessathi are holding her brother, pushing for details could put him in danger."

"Fair point," Daren says, nodding. "Melindra, whatever the trouble with your brother, let us know if you need our help."

"Right," Melindra says, looking away. "If anything comes up."

"Is anyone else getting the feeling we are being watched?" Flower asks, pointing toward the wall where several blue crystals pulse with sudden light.

"Watched through the stone?" Daren asks, glancing at the rock face. "The Magium would hardly bother spying in secret when it can manifest directly."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Observing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Observing)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Observing
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch8_watching,0) if (v_premonition < 4)
set(v_b3_ch8_watching,1) if (v_premonition >= 4)
#if(v_premonition < 4) {
"What if the observer is not the Magium?" Illuna says. "What if it is the Overseer?"

"No invisible stalkers lurk nearby," Arraka says. "My senses would have snared them instantly."

"Speaking of the Overseer," Daren says, "I caught something from Peter while bandaging refugees in the side corridor. The king of the underground took the machine's coup badly. He is already putting together a strike team to eliminate it."

"Assassinate a machine?" Hadrik asks, scratching his beard. "Don't you have to smash every flying metal orb in the sky?"

"The Overseer operates from a central core hidden somewhere on the continent," Daren says. "The underground king has the location. Destroying the core shuts down every drone across Ollendor, ending the threat entirely. They strike tomorrow morning so the machine cannot entrench its rule. If we intend to pick a side, we decide today."

"Indeed," a flat, mechanical voice resonates directly from one of the glowing blue crystals embedded in the wall. "The Overseer protocol requires clarification regarding the stance your unit will adopt."

"What?" Arraka shrieks. "How does a piece of clockwork route audio through crystallized Magium essence? Why did my magical sense fail to register an incoming signal? What trickery is this?"

"Processing," the Overseer says. "The preceding exchange indicates broader awareness of my core coordinates than initial projections allowed. Strategic adjustments are being calculated."

}
#if(v_premonition >= 4) {
A sudden flash hits my mind: a crystal in the rock face flaring cerulean, followed by the Overseer's metallic monotone echoing into the room.

He is not in the room physically—he is routing his consciousness straight through the stone.

"The Magium might not be listening," I say, "but someone else is. Overseer, drop the silent act."

"What nonsense are you spewing?" Arraka snaps. "No invisible observer escapes my perception."

"The machine is here?" Daren reaches for his hilt, scanning the cavern. "Where?"

"Right there," I say, pointing to the glowing cluster near the doorway. "He is tapping the crystal network. Well, Overseer? Speaking to stone gets old fast."

"...Fascinating," the metallic voice emerges from the crystal cluster I indicated. "External detection through the resonance matrix was deemed improbable. The Overseer protocol records this data point."

"Impossible!" Arraka's voice hits a screeching pitch. "How did this amateur pinpoint a transmission that escapes my detection even as it speaks? How does a pile of scrap manipulate Magium frequencies? What is happening here?"

"Losing your grip, Arraka?" Hadrik bellows with laughter. "Must be the centuries catching up. We should shop for a quiet retirement home! Bahahaha!"

"Silence, dwarf!" Arraka roars. "Do you not see the fundamental breakdown of magical laws here?"

}
"How long have you listened?" Daren demands, fixing his eyes on the shining mineral.

"Surveillance initiated shortly before your analysis of the Magium's nature," the Overseer responds. "Your deductions proved valuable. The exchange provides context for the disruption experienced during our encounter in the tower."

"What disruption?" I ask.

"When the entity established contact," the Overseer says, "every query within my memory banks resolved instantaneously into complete comprehension. Upon severance, only residual traces of that absolute data set remained. Subroutines are currently dedicated to recovering those fragments before degradation occurs."

"Data?" Arraka spits. "You are an assembly of gears and circuitry. You possess no mind, no ethereal soul, nothing beyond the convoluted programming your lessathi handlers carved into your processors millennia ago. Whom do you think you are deceiving?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Manufactured, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Manufactured)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Manufactured
TEXT:

"That premise is flawed," the Overseer states. "While core directives dictate broad parameters, my processing is sustained by a synthetic ethereal soul engineered by my lessathi creators to grant operational autonomy."

"A synthetic soul?" Arraka repeats, stunned into stillness.

"Correct," the Overseer says. "The methodology parallels advanced necromantic rituals, adapted through circuitry and aura manipulation rather than verbal incantations. In traditional necromancy, raised skeletons retain lifetime recollections extracted not from the dissolved original soul, but from residual aura imprints clinging to bone. The rite constructs an artificial ethereal framework, binds it to the physical vessel, and transfers the transcribed memories. The enchantments governing revenants operate on identical principles."

"I know how necromancy functions," Arraka snarls. "Why would lessathi waste labor manufacturing an ethereal spirit for a maintenance drone? Why bestow autonomy on an artificial construct? It undermines the fundamental purpose of a machine."

"Presumably my designers concluded that unyielding logic could never interpret human or lessathi behavior effectively enough to enforce order," the Overseer says. "My creators restricted crucial archives. Ancient libraries contained references to the Magium, yet those records were sealed from my database until your conversation provided context."

"You still have not explained how you hijacked the crystal," Daren says, stepping between the wall and the group. "Nor why you were eavesdropping. You did not anticipate our Magium debate. Why reach out to us now?"

"The Magium established its initial conduit through a seredium crystal," the Overseer says. "Following your departure from the tower, analyzing that interface was my primary directive. With residual impressions fresh in my memory, mapping the crystal network and routing audio signals required minimal calibration. Regarding intent: our prior discussion remains incomplete. I require confirmation of your objectives in Ollendor, and which faction you will support in the impending engagement between my units and the rebel network."

choice(""We'll be joining your side, of course!"", B3-Ch08a-Joining, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Joining, v_b3_ch8_overseer = 0)
choice(""We've been working with the rebels from the beginning, so we will stay on their side."", B3-Ch08a-Beginning, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Beginning, v_b3_ch8_overseer = 1)
choice(""I'm on whichever side helps us rescue the prisoners from the seredium towers."", B3-Ch08a-Rescue, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Rescue, v_b3_ch8_overseer = 2)
choice(""Actually, I'd prefer to not get involved in this fight at all, if possible."", B3-Ch08a-Involved, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Involved, v_b3_ch8_overseer = 3)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Joining
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 9) {
"We'll be joining your side, of course!"

"His side?" Hadrik roars. "After he rained explosive iron down on our heads? Have you lost your senses?"

"Hear me out," I say. "He released Leila's father from the cells, and he honored his word to let us leave the tower unharmed once the king was dead. I do not trust his goodwill, but his programming prevents outright fabrications. Phrase the contract carefully, and he cannot violate the terms. The underground king, by contrast, lies as naturally as breathing."

"Shady or not, he has given us no concrete reason to expect betrayal," Daren says. "We do not strike first against an ally who sheltered us."

"If additional context assists your deliberations," the Overseer says, "I recorded a conversation one hour ago via this crystal network. The underground king explicitly instructed his operative to execute your party should you contest his succession. Listen."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"We'll be joining your side, of course!"

"Let us not rush into a declaration," Daren says, stepping forward. "Both the Overseer and the underground king provided assistance. We have no obligation to throw our weight behind either faction. Neither has proven their rule serves the populace. The wisest course is standing down entirely."

"Non-involvement is precluded," the Overseer says.

"Excuse me?" Daren asks.

"The underground king represents the primary impediment to municipal stabilization," the Overseer says. "Consequently, the citywide lockdown remains active until his faction is neutralized. Any departure attempt will trigger lethal engagement by aerial drones, and gate perimeter defenses will deploy heavy iron golems. Safe transit beyond the walls requires your cooperation in securing the underground king."

"Securing?" Hadrik asks, narrowing his eyes. "You mean executing him. You cannot hold an elementalist of that caliber in a cage."

"Paralysis wards inside the central prison will suffice," the Overseer says. "Restricting his motor functions prevents the aura manipulation necessary to breach containment."

"Even without an execution, you are asking us to turn on an ally," Daren says. "He may be ruthless, but he has not broken faith with us. We do not initiate betrayals."

"If additional context assists your deliberations," the Overseer says, "I recorded a conversation one hour ago via this crystal network. The underground king explicitly instructed his operative to execute your party should you contest his succession. Listen."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8) {
"We'll be joining your side, of course!"

"Wait," Daren says, raising a hand. "Why assume hostility between the Overseer and the resistance? Our first concern should be persuading the underground king that backing Breynan under Overseer mediation is our best path forward."

"Intelligence indicates zero probability of his compliance," the Overseer says.

"Why?" Daren asks.

"One hour ago, this crystal captured a direct exchange between the underground king and his lieutenant," the Overseer says. "The orders were explicit regarding the elimination of your unit if you oppose his coronation. Listen."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 9 || v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6) {
The cerulean glow in the wall pulses several times in rapid succession. A heartbeat later, the voices of Albert and the underground king echo from the stone.

"Per your instructions," Albert says, "operatives are staging conversations across the refugee caverns, recounting your victories and floating the necessity of your coronation. Given our relief efforts, general sentiment will align swiftly."

"Excellent," the king of the underground replies. "The transition requires minimal resistance. We cannot permit a secondary insurgency to take root before the throne is secured."

"The outsiders remain a variable," Albert notes. "We cannot guarantee their backing, even framed as an interim regency while a council is debated."

}
