ID: B3-Ch08a-Antagonizing
TEXT:

"Careful," the king's voice purrs from the stone. "We avoid antagonizing Daren and his companions openly. The healer commands genuine respect among the refugees after his work in the tunnels; moving against him in public damages our standing. That said, the governance of Ollendor will not hinge on travelers who leave tomorrow and bear none of the consequences. If they resist my appointment, we persuade them that no other viable candidate exists."

"And if persuasion fails?" Albert asks.

"A simple disagreement warrants no retaliation," the king says. "Turning them hostile over ideology is wasteful. But if they actively obstruct my ascension... well, you have resolved delicate problems in the past. Handle it in your usual manner."

"Efficiently?" Albert asks.

"Discreetly," the king corrects.

"As you command, my king."

The light inside the crystal fades to a dull simmer.

"Backstabbing hypocrite!" Hadrik spits, his fist slamming into his palm. "That is our reward for clearing his path? Ordering his pet assassin to slit our throats the moment we disagree?"

#if(v_b3_ch8_watching == 0) {
"He did not... explicitly state that Albert should kill us," Daren says, though his voice lacks conviction. "It could imply a financial buyout or an escort out of the territory."

"He told his chief executioner to solve the problem the way he always solves problems," Kate says coldly. "Do not delude yourself, Daren. The king intends to eliminate us if we challenge his throne. 'Discreetly' means avoiding a spectacle before his supporters. An ambush on the surface, staged to look like Overseer drones or city guard crossfire, solves his problem cleanly."

"Precisely," the Overseer says from the wall. "My projections indicate the underground king will launch an assault to seize complete control of Ollendor within twenty-four hours. He will request your participation as nominal allies. The optimal window for his betrayal falls during that operation. If you partner with my administration instead, we can coordinate an ambush and neutralize his vanguard before he executes his plan."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_watching == 1) {
"He did not... explicitly state that Albert should kill us," Daren says, though his voice lacks conviction. "It could imply a financial buyout or an escort out of the territory."

"He told his chief executioner to solve the problem the way he always solves problems," Kate says coldly. "Do not delude yourself, Daren. The king intends to eliminate us if we challenge his throne. 'Discreetly' means avoiding a spectacle before his supporters. An ambush on the surface, staged to look like Overseer drones or city guard crossfire, solves his problem cleanly."

"Precisely," the Overseer says from the wall. "When the underground king launches his offensive, he will request your participation as nominal allies. The optimal window for his betrayal falls during that operation. If you partner with my administration instead, we can coordinate an ambush and neutralize his vanguard before he executes his plan."

}
"In short," Hadrik says, "you want us to agree to his surface raid, lead him into position, and spring your trap?"

"Affirmative," the Overseer replies. "Immediate commitment is not required. I grant you time to confer and establish consensus. The underground king cannot mobilize before tomorrow morning; I shall re-establish contact through the network this evening. If no further inquiries remain, I disconnect."

"Right," Daren says reluctantly. "Goodbye for now."

"Farewell," the mechanical voice recedes, and the crystal's glow fades entirely.

"That was illuminating," Daren sighs, looking around at the circle. "Where do we go from here?"

choice(""I'm fine with anything, as long as we get away from these crystals."", B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_b3_ch8_next = 0)
choice(""We should have a discussion with the rebels about who should be the next king."", B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_b3_ch8_next = 1)
choice(""We should ask the king what he is planning to do about the seredium tower prisoners."", B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Flaming, v_b3_ch8_next = 2)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Flaming
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_next == 0) {
"I'm fine with anything, as long as we get away from these crystals."

"Seconded," Hadrik says. "Let us find a corner where we can debate without a machine recording every breath."

"The waiting room should be clear," Daren says. "The concussive blasts ripped the crystal clusters out of the masonry, and the earth mages never bothered re-seeding them during the repairs."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_next == 1) {
"We should have a discussion with the rebels about who should be the next king."

"We should," Daren says. "If we rally support around an alternative candidate before his operatives finish their campaign, he may find his claim untenable."

"The underground king does not strike me as someone who yields power to public sentiment," Kate notes dryly.

"Perhaps not," Daren says, "but his own words showed he fears an unstable transition. Shifting opinion makes unilateral moves far riskier for him. Let us move to the waiting room and gather intel."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_next == 2) {
"We should ask the king what he is planning to do about the seredium tower prisoners."

"A necessary conversation," Kate says. "I intended to confront him regarding the towers the moment we crossed paths."

"We move to the waiting room, then," Daren says. "Peter or Albert should be directing movement near the main tunnel; they can direct us to the king."

}
"I know this will likely be dismissed," Melindra says as we step into the corridor, "but why does our group care what happens to this city once we leave? Half of you did not know Ollendor existed until I drew the map. Why does it matter if the whole place burns to ash behind us, so long as we are clear of the smoke?"

"Not everyone is departing," Illuna says, her expression cold. "Leila and doctor Reidzek intend to settle here. If the city burns, they burn with it."

"Right," Melindra says, glancing at Leila. "A valid consideration. So which faction receives your swords?"

"Undecided," Daren says. "I want to gauge the underground king's reaction to an alternate candidate before committing to a confrontation."

"Understood," Melindra says with a faint shrug. "Best of luck with that."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Weakling, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Weakling) if (v_b3_ch8_watching == 1)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Break, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Break) if (v_b3_ch8_watching == 0)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Weakling
TEXT:

We follow the winding path toward the waiting area until the rock walls give way to a torchlit corridor devoid of blue crystals. The moment the last vein of seredium vanishes behind us, Arraka's voice crackles from the gold casing.

"Finally," she snaps. "Outside signal range. Now explain yourself, weakling: how did you pinpoint the Overseer's presence when my own detection registered nothing?"

"That has been festering the entire walk?" Kate asks, giving the amulet a dry glance. "Really?"

"Ask with appropriate courtesy," I say, "and I might consider sharing."

"Do not insult me," Arraka hisses. "You used a contrivance. An amateur with your dormant channels could not have tracked that frequency through pure aura perception."

choice(""That is precisely how I detected him, though."", B3-Ch08a-Detected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Detected)
choice(""Of course I didn't detect him by sensing his aura."", B3-Ch08a-Sensing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Sensing)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Detected
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch8_bluffer,1) if (v_bluff >= 4)
set(v_b3_ch8_precisely,1) if (v_bluff < 4)
set(v_b3_ch8_precisely,2) if (v_bluff >= 4)
achievement("Master of bluffing",v_ac_b3_ch8_bluffer)
#if(v_bluff < 4) {
"That is precisely how I detected him, though."

"Your aura is trembling," Arraka says. "You are bluffing. If not magical sense, then what? Lessathi bio-mechanics? Or one of your device functions? What were those diagnostic parameters again? Ancient Languages, Observation, Hearing... Premonition. Of course. You caught a future sensory impression of the transmission. That explains the accuracy."

"Took you long enough," Illuna murmurs.

"I have no interest in lessathi trinkets," Arraka snaps. "Do you expect me to keep a catalog of his obscure settings memorized? He should be grateful I deciphered his hidden aura parameters in the first place."

"Can we move past my device settings?" I ask. "I would prefer not listening to a lecture the entire way to the hall."

}
#if(v_bluff >= 4) {
"That is precisely how I detected him, though."

"You did what?" Arraka shrieks. "An unawakened amateur reading subtle crystal resonance? It was a coincidence! Try it again, you pathetic excuse for a—"

"Be quiet," Illuna cuts in, her tone flat. "He is lying. The only method capable of such precision without active casting is premonition. Correct, Barry?"

"You ruin all the best setups, Illuna," I sigh.

"Premonition," Arraka says, the rage instantly draining into smug relief. "Naturally. It was obvious from the start."

"Good to know," I say with a grin. "Even with five thousand years of aura mastery, strong emotion disrupts your focus enough to swallow a bluff. I will file that away."

"I would not recommend testing it, Barry-boy," Arraka warns, her voice dropping an octave.

"Can we change the subject before blood gets drawn?" Kate asks.

}
"Ahem," doctor Reidzek clears his throat, breaking the rhythm of our footsteps. "If I may interject... the theoretical debate regarding the Magium was utterly engrossing. For a low-tier lessathi like myself, lore surrounding the entity was relegated to folklore. Witnessing such high-level discourse, even as an observer, proved remarkably exhilarating."

"I wish I shared your enthusiasm, doctor," Daren says with a weary sigh. "All these revelations regarding fragments and cosmic scale have done is deepen my doubts about the tournament prize itself."

"In what manner?" Reidzek asks.

"When the heralds promised 'access' to the Magium," Daren says, "I assumed a manageable boon. Now I picture the organizers marching the victor in front of a towering seredium monolith and demanding an aura link. A grandmaster like Azarius might navigate that abyss, but with my training... I am hopelessly out of my depth."

A harsh laugh erupts from the amulet.

"You are only realizing this now, paladin?" Arraka cackles. "What did you envision? A golden chalice with a toggle switch? Is that what lured you across the sea?"

"Yes," Daren snaps. "Why wouldn't I? It is billed as a prize for mortal champions, not a cosmic landmine capable of unmaking the continent if mishandled."

"The Varathian kings drafted the proclamation without the faintest grasp of what they were advertising," Arraka says. "And the lessathi council pulling the strings had zero incentive to enlighten them. Local politics bore me, but the dynamic is obvious: both factions are concealing their true objectives, each convinced they hold the leverage. They engineered this tournament for their own covert ends, and you simpletons are marching to their drum without questioning the tune."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Collected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Collected)


