"You are only realizing this now, paladin?" Arraka cackles. "What did you envision? A golden chalice with a toggle switch? Is that what lured you across the sea?"

"Yes," Daren snaps. "Why wouldn't I? It is billed as a prize for mortal champions, not a cosmic landmine capable of unmaking the continent if mishandled."

"The Varathian kings drafted the proclamation without the faintest grasp of what they were advertising," Arraka says. "And the lessathi council pulling the strings had zero incentive to enlighten them. Local politics bore me, but the dynamic is obvious: both factions are concealing their true objectives, each convinced they hold the leverage. They engineered this tournament for their own covert ends, and you simpletons are marching to their drum without questioning the tune."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch08a-Collected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch08a-Collected)


ID: B3-Ch08a-Collected
TEXT:

"So the rulers knew from the start that most mages couldn't touch the prize, and invited us anyway?" Hadrik's voice booms down the tunnel. "That is worse than sending us after invisible pinecones only stillwaters can trace. Do those monarchs care so little for the hundreds of fighters butchered in their arena for a phantom reward?"

"Perhaps that was the design from the opening bell," Illuna says, her tone analytical. "Drawing the preeminent mages of the outer continents to Varathia, encouraging them to slaughter one another, and stripping foreign nations of their elite defenders. An invasion across the seas after the tournament concludes would face minimal organized resistance."

"Or they feared an invasion themselves," I say, "and orchestrated a preemptive cull of foreign talent. That would align with their requirement that the champion pledge to defend Varathia against external threats."

"A plausible alternative," Illuna concedes.

"What of the lessathi administrators?" Daren asks. "What is their stake in this bloodbath?"

"Exploiting the turmoil to seize political control of the city-states," Kate suggests. "Or using the monarchs' access to pinpoint the exact chamber where the Magium connects to this plane, intending to claim the nexus themselves. Without intelligence on their internal factions, we are guessing."

"Your friend Diane served in Meridith's vanguard," I say to Kate. "We can press her for details once we reconnect. She might have overheard command directives."

"We will see," Kate says, looking straight ahead.

"I have to admit," Daren says, turning to study me, "your composure surprises me, Barry. No one here pursued the Magium with your single-mindedness. How are you so undisturbed by the possibility that your goal is unusable?"

"Kate and I already worked through this," I say. "When my resolve cracked after the trial with the God of Time, she mapped out an alternate route for turning myself into a mage."

"Did she?" Daren glances at Kate with genuine respect before looking back at me. "And what does this contingency entail?"

"First, unlock the magical stats on my booster so I have the temporary channels needed to bridge the Magium interface," I say. "Once I tap into that knowledge base, I can engineer a modification to my device, rewiring it to draw energy straight from the magical plane instead of siphoning nearby casters. That turns me into a permanent, self-sufficient mage. It is an intricate workaround, but the logic holds."

"Right," Daren says, a faint smile touching his face. "I cannot speak to the technical feasibility, but your optimism is welcome."

